🚀 PROBLEM: REORGANIZE STRING
🔹 Problem Statement

Given a string s, rearrange the characters so that no two adjacent characters are the same.

Return any valid arrangement.
If it's not possible, return an empty string "".

Example
Input:  s = "aab"
Output: "aba"

Input: s = "aaab"
Output: ""

🔹 Why Interviewers LOVE this problem

✔ Tests Greedy + Heap (Priority Queue)
✔ Frequency counting logic
✔ Edge-case reasoning
✔ Appears in Amazon, Google, Meta

🔹 Key Observation 💡

If any character appears more than (n+1)/2 times,
👉 Reorganization is impossible.

🔹 Algorithm (Greedy + Max Heap)

Count frequency of each character

Add all characters to Max Heap (by frequency)

While heap size > 1:

Take top two most frequent characters

Append both to result

Decrease their frequency

Push back if still remaining

If one character is left:

If freq > 1 → return ""

Else append it

🔹 Dry Run Example 🧠
Input:
s = "aab"

Frequency Map:
a → 2
b → 1

Heap processing:
Take a, b → "ab"
Remaining a → add at end → "aba"


✅ Valid output

🔹 Java Code (Interview Standard)
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public String reorganizeString(String s) {

        // Step 1: Frequency count
        int[] freq = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray())
            freq[c - 'a']++;

        // Step 2: Max Heap based on frequency
        PriorityQueue<Character> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> freq[b - 'a'] - freq[a - 'a']
        );

        // Step 3: Add characters to heap
        for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
            if (freq[c - 'a'] > 0)
                pq.offer(c);
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();

        // Step 4: Greedy pairing
        while (pq.size() > 1) {
            char first = pq.poll();
            char second = pq.poll();

            result.append(first);
            result.append(second);

            freq[first - 'a']--;
            freq[second - 'a']--;

            if (freq[first - 'a'] > 0)
                pq.offer(first);
            if (freq[second - 'a'] > 0)
                pq.offer(second);
        }

        // Step 5: Handle remaining character
        if (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            char last = pq.poll();
            if (freq[last - 'a'] > 1)
                return "";
            result.append(last);
        }

        return result.toString();
    }
}

🔹 Step-by-Step Explanation 🪜
Why take two characters at a time?

➡ Prevents same characters being adjacent
➡ Always uses highest frequency first

Why Max Heap?

➡ So the character that causes maximum conflict is placed early

🔹 Time & Space Complexity
Metric	Complexity
Time	O(N log 26) ≈ O(N)
Space	O(26)
